x using fmt.\n\nSimplest implementation: \n```python\ndef format(self, record):\n    message = record.getMessage()\n    if self.max_message_chars is not None and getattr(record, \"keep_full_message\", None) is not True:\n        message = _truncate_message(message, self.max_message_chars)\n    record.message = message\n    ... then super().format(record) uses record.message via (record.message or record.getMessage())\n```\nBut mutating record.message is mutating the shared record's attribute? Requirement says cannot overwrite msg, args, exc_info, stack_info. record.message is set during formatting by other handlers though; logging.Formatter.format sets record.message = record.getMessage(). So setting it is what logging does anyway. But then a subsequent full handler would call format() again which recomputes record.message = record.getMessage() — fine for full handler. But for our truncation we'd set record.message truncated; safer to not permanently mutate: save and restore.\n\nBetter: implement own formatting to avoid mutating:\n\n```python\ndef format(self, record):\n    record.message = record.getMessage()  # base does this\n    ...\n```\nAlternative safe approach: copy record? Simpler: compute truncated message, then temporarily set record.message and record.msg? Hmm.\n\nCleanest: replicate Formatter.format body but with truncated message:\n\n```python\ndef format(self, record):\n    if self.max_message_chars is None or getattr(record, \"keep_full_message\", None) is True:\n        msg = super().format(record)\n    else:\n        record = _copy_with_truncated_message(record, self.max_message_chars)\n        msg = super().format(record)\n    # multi-line prefixing and color\n```\nA shallow copy via copy.copy(record) — need import copy? We can do that; standard lib allowed. But copy.copy on LogRecord works (shallow copy of instance dict). Then set msg on copy to truncated, args=() . But exc_info etc. shared — fine, super().format handles exc_text caching; caching